# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-bridge-com-error-dropped-on-ansi — the COM error the bridge recorded never reached the message, on a French Windows
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: next-work

* Symptom

=verify:epure-api:windows= on MR !336 (2026-09-28, job 16774481586): all
three AutoCAD runs failed the same way, after about 122 s each:

#+begin_example
alfe: alfe AUTOCAD backend error (phase BOOTSTRAP): AutoCAD COM bridge
  (cscript) exited before READY (exit 4): ATTACHED=0
CREATED=0 [PROCESS-EXITED-BEFORE-READY]
#+end_example

=ATTACHED=0 CREATED=0= and exit 4 means the bridge's =CreateObject= failed;
the bridge then writes =COM.STAGE=, =COM.ERROR.HEX= and
=COM.ERROR.DESCRIPTION= to its error file, and =summarize-process-exit=
prints them (alfe-autocad-cad-selection-ignores-com-progid). None of them
appeared. The same shape on MR !330 the day before.

* Cause

The bridge writes that file with the FileSystemObject's default, the ANSI code
page -- CP1252 on the runner's French Windows -- so an accented COM description
("L'exécution du serveur a échoué") is not UTF-8. alfe read it with
=uiop:read-file-string= (UTF-8) inside =ignore-errors=: the first accented byte
signalled, the error became NIL, and the message fell back to cscript's stdout.
The one line that explains the failure was the one that got dropped.

The existing test (=autocad-bridge-exit-message-reports-the-com-failure=)
passed the error text as a string, so it never exercised the read.

* Resolution — alfe 2.2.146

=read-bridge-errors= decodes the file's octets with babel: strict UTF-8 first
(what alfe itself writes there), CP1252 when that fails. From octets rather than
through a stream's external format because CCL, unlike SBCL, substitutes U+FFFD
for an invalid UTF-8 byte instead of signalling, so a stream-based fallback would
never fall back there. Test =autocad-bridge-errors-are-read-in-the-ansi-code-page-too=
writes the CP1252 bytes and checks the file read, the message (=0x80080005=, the
accented description), that a UTF-8 file still reads as UTF-8, and that an
absent file is NIL. alfe suite 1358/1358 on SBCL and CCL.

What the failure itself is -- why =CreateObject= fails on the runner now -- is
=alfe-autocad-createobject-fails-on-runner=; the next AutoCAD run will print it.
